{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786481943,"nonce":"M9rppdzaCHGN3RqFN8hq8pidVKI=","title":"**What is wrong.** CONTEXT.md §5b says the repository *\"is the single place SQLSTATEs become `errs.Kind`\"*, and SPEC §L.9(2) is more specific still: *\"Translate SQLSTATEs into `errs.Kind` in exactly one helper:\"* followed by the eight-row table. There are **nine** helpers, each named `mapErr`, and they do not agree. Five implement the whole table. Three implement three rows and let everything else fall through to `errs.KindInternal`, which is a 500. One implements none.","message":"**The demonstration.** Nine `func mapErr`, and what each covers:\n\n- Full table (`23505`, `23503`, `23514`, `23502`, `40001`/`40P01`, `57014`, `53300`): `internal/alerts/repository/mapper.go:54`, `internal/grouping/repository/mapper.go:41`, `internal/sources/repository/errors.go:41`, `internal/channels/repository/errors.go:39`, `internal/identity/repository/errors.go:23`.\n- Three rows only (`23505`, `23503`, `23514`), everything else → `errs.KindInternal`: `internal/notification/repository/errors.go:48` (fallthrough at `:74`), `internal/streaming/repository/events.go:210` (fallthrough at `:227`).\n- No SQLSTATE handling at all: `internal/drill/repository/drills.go:337` — `pgx.ErrNoRows` then `errs.Wrap(err, errs.KindInternal, \"drill_query_failed\", …)`.\n- A deliberate exception that must stay: `internal/ingestion/repository/errors.go:24` also handles only three rows, but its fallthrough at `:43` is `errs.KindUnavailable`, and the ⚠️ note at `:18-23` explains why — *\"Everything here except NotFound must stay on a kind that the ingest handler maps to 503.\"* This one is right and is the model.\n\nThe `57014` row of the §L.9 table reads: *\"| `57014` query_canceled (statement_timeout) | overload | `KindUnavailable`, `Retryable: true` |\"*. `alerts` obeys it at `mapper.go:80`, `grouping` at `:68`, `sources` at `:68`, `channels` at `:66`, `identity` at `:46`. `notification`, `streaming` and `drill` return 500 instead — and this is not a theoretical path, because **every** pool sets the timeout: `internal/platform/db/pool.go:53` writes `statement_timeout` as a runtime parameter on every connection, with `ingest_statement_timeout: 2s` and `general_statement_timeout: 15s` shipped at `deploy/helm/oto/values.yaml:411-412`. A slow Postgres produces `57014` in every module, not just on the ingest path.\n\nThe fallback error code is spelled three ways on top of that. `constraintCode(pg *pgconn.PgError, fallback string) string` is duplicated verbatim at `internal/ingestion/repository/errors.go:50-55` and `internal/identity/repository/errors.go:60-65`. Four files inline a second spelling — `code := pg.ConstraintName; if code == \"\" { code = \"sqlstate_\" + pg.Code }` (`alerts/repository/mapper.go:64-67`, `grouping/repository/mapper.go:51-54`, `sources/repository/errors.go:51-54`, `channels/repository/errors.go:49-52`). `notification/repository/errors.go:58-61` inlines a third, substituting the literal `\"constraint\"`. `streaming` and `drill` are a fourth behaviour: they discard the constraint name entirely and hardcode `\"ui_event_conflict\"`, `\"ui_event_fk\"`, `\"drill_query_failed\"` — which contradicts CONTEXT.md §6: *\"**Constraint and index names are a runtime contract** — §L.9 returns them as `errs.Error.Code` on 23505/23503.\"*\n\n**Why it matters.** A 500 and a 503 are different promises. CONTEXT.md §5b's taxonomy line is explicit about which one overload gets: *\"Error taxonomy: validation (422) · conflict (409) · precondition (412, wrong entity state) · upstream-failure (502/504) · unavailable (503, our backpressure).\"* A statement timeout is our backpressure, and a 503 carries `Retry-After` and tells a client to come back; a 500 tells it something is broken and pages somebody. Three modules therefore mistranslate a busy database into a defect, and they lose the retryability with it.\n\nThe deeper problem is that a nine-way copy cannot be corrected once. A new row in the §L.9 table — or a change to one that exists — has to land in nine files, and the four already-divergent ones prove that it will not.\n\n**Where.** `internal/alerts/repository/mapper.go:54-89`, `internal/grouping/repository/mapper.go:41-77`, `internal/sources/repository/errors.go:41-77`, `internal/channels/repository/errors.go:39-75`, `internal/identity/repository/errors.go:23-65`, `internal/notification/repository/errors.go:48-76`, `internal/streaming/repository/events.go:210-228`, `internal/drill/repository/drills.go:337-342`, `internal/ingestion/repository/errors.go:24-55`.\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** One helper in `internal/platform/db` owns the §L.9 table, and each repository passes only what is genuinely its own: its not-found code, its query-failed code prefix, and — for `ingestion` alone — the never-a-4xx fallthrough kind. All nine `mapErr` bodies become calls to it, `streaming` and `drill` start returning constraint names, and a test asserts the full table once rather than nine times.","files":null}]}